    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "id": "overview",
      "content": "## Overview\n\nThe purpose of a duct silencer is to reduce the noise from the air-handling system caused by:\n- Fan operation\n- Passage of air through straight ducts\n- Impact of air flowing through components such as elbows, branches, mixing boxes, etc.\n\n### Key Silencer Classifications\n\n**Absorptive/Dissipative Silencers**: Use sound absorbing materials to attenuate sound waves. Widely used in HVAC duct systems with parallel baffle arrangements.\n\n**Reflective/Reactive Silencers**: Reflect sound waves back to the source using tuned cavities or membranes. Rarely used in HVAC systems.\n\n**Diffuser/Depressive Silencers**: Use perforated elements to slow flow velocity and prevent low frequency noise generation.\n\n**Active Silencers**: Use electro-acoustical means to cancel noise. Effective below 300 Hz for steady noise fields."
